 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** The temporal encoding uses high-frequency sinusoids. Care must be taken with floating-point precision for large $t$ values to avoid numerical instability in the sine/cosine calculations [S1].
-*   **Covariance Validity:** The refined covariance matrix $\hat{\Sigma}$ must remain positive semi-definite. Since $\hat{\Sigma}$ is reconstructed from $\hat{L}\hat{L}^\top$, it is inherently positive semi-definite by construction, provided $\hat{L}$ is a valid lower-triangular matrix [S1]. However, the residuals $r_l$ must be small enough to prevent $\hat{L}$ from becoming numerically unstable or degenerate.
-*   **MLP Training:** The MLPs $\phi$ must be trained jointly with the Gaussian parameters. If the residuals are not properly regularized, they may lead to overfitting or unstable dynamics [S1].
+*   **Precision:** The temporal encoding involves high-frequency sinusoids. Care must be taken with numerical stability for large $t$ values, although the periodic nature mitigates overflow.
+*   **Covariance Validity:** The refined covariance $\hat{\Sigma}$ must remain positive semi-definite. Since $\hat{\Sigma} = \hat{L}\hat{L}^\top$, it is inherently positive semi-definite by construction, provided $\hat{L}$ is real [S1]. However, if the residual $\phi_l(f)$ is large, the resulting covariance might become ill-conditioned or numerically unstable during subsequent operations (e.g., inversion in conditional slicing) [S1].
+*   **Timing:** The refinement module is applied *before* the conditional slicing step [S1]. Applying it after would be incorrect as the slicing depends on the refined 7D parameters.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds the cost of two MLP forward passes per Gaussian per view. The MLPs are small ($C_{in} \times 64 \times C_{out}$), so the overhead is relatively low compared to the rendering cost, preserving the real-time efficiency of 3DGS [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the weights of the MLPs. The number of parameters is proportional to $C_{in} \times 64 + 64 \times C_{out}$ per MLP. Since there are four MLPs, the memory overhead is constant per Gaussian instance (shared weights) or small if weights are shared across Gaussians.
-*   **Strengths:** Enables accurate modeling of complex non-rigid deformations and time-varying appearance without increasing the number of Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires training additional neural networks. Performance depends on the capacity of the MLPs and the quality of the temporal encoding.
-*   **When to use:** When rendering dynamic scenes with non-rigid deformations or time-varying appearance where static Gaussians are insufficient [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** For static scenes, where standard 3DGS or densification strategies are more appropriate.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds the cost of evaluating four small MLPs per Gaussian per frame. The MLPs are $C_{in} \times 64 \times C_{out}$, which is negligible compared to the rasterization cost.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the MLP weights. The MLPs are small (hidden size 64), so the memory overhead is minimal compared to the Gaussian parameters themselves.
+*   **Strengths:** Enables accurate modeling of complex non-rigid deformations and time-varying appearance without increasing the number of Gaussians [S1]. Preserves the computational efficiency of real-time rendering pipelines [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the capacity of the MLPs to capture the dynamics. May struggle with highly chaotic or unpredictable motions if the MLP is not sufficiently expressive or trained adequately.
+*   **When to use:** When rendering dynamic scenes with non-rigid deformations or time-varying appearance where static Gaussians fail to capture the motion [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** For static scenes where standard 3DGS is sufficient, as the MLP overhead is unnecessary.
 
 ## Background
-Adaptive Gaussian Refinement was introduced in the 7DGS paper (Unified Spatial-Temporal-Angular Gaussian Splatting) [S1]. It was developed to address the limitations of static covariance in representing complex dynamic behaviors such as non-rigid deformations or motion-induced shape changes [S1]. The method was evaluated on synthetic monocular videos (D-NeRF) and in-the-wild multi-view videos (Technicolor) [S1].
+Adaptive Gaussian Refinement was introduced in the 7DGS (Unified Spatial-Temporal-Angular Gaussian Splatting) paper [S1]. It was developed to address the limitation of static covariance in 3D Gaussian Splatting when representing complex dynamic behaviors such as non-rigid deformations or motion-induced shape changes [S1]. The method is part of the 7DGS model, which jointly encodes spatial structure, temporal evolution, and angular information [S1].
 
